WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.680
Iran is preparing to charge fees for ships passing the Strait of Hormuz, a route that carries roughly

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:12.960
one-fifth of global oil trade. This is Omni View, episode 156. Iranian officials have

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.860
begun steps to impose fees on vessels transiting the Strait. They cite Turkey's long-standing

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:23.900
system of charges for passage through the Bosporus and Dardanelles as a workable precedent.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.640
The Strait links the Persian Gulf to the open ocean and remains a vital corridor for oil

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.420
tankers and other hermersial shipping.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.620
Nikkeh Asia reports the development as part of Iran's effort to generate revenue and assert

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:43.420
control over a critical choke point. Regional analysts note that any fee system would require

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:49.260
coordination with Oman, which shares the Strait's southern shore. Shipping companies and energy

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:54.120
importers have historically treated the Strait as a free passage under long-standing custom.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:00.320
Iran frames the change as an exercise of sovereign rights similar to those exercised by Turkey.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:06.500
The proposal arrives amid broader tensions over energy markets and sanctions. Observers

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:12.220
will watch whether Iran issues formal regulations and how Oman and major oil importers respond.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:19.120
Shifting now to Thailand. Violence has intensified in Thailand's southern provinces, with renewed

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:24.620
attacks reported in areas long-affected by separatist activity. The latest incidents have

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:30.080
involved both armed clashes and civilian impacts in districts that have seen periodic unrest for

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:36.540
years. Security forces have responded with increased patrols and operations. Local schools and

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:42.400
markets have experienced disruptions as a result. Nikkeh Asia describes the flare-up as the sharpest

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:48.360
in recent months, involving both insurgent groups and security forces. Local communities have

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:53.960
faced repeated cycles of violence that disrupt daily life and schooling. Taya authorities have

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:59.420
maintained that development programs and dialogue remain central to their approach. Some residents

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:05.020
question whether those measures address underlying grievances. The pattern of escalation and response

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:10.600
has repeated across multiple administrations. International observers continue to monitor the

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:16.200
human cost in the affected border regions. The government's next security and dialogue steps will

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:22.740
be tracked by regional monitors. Next, a dispute in international sports governance. UFFA has

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:28.900
initiated criminal proceedings against FIFA and its president Gianni Infantino over a failed plan

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:34.540
to sell stakes in the World Cup. The action centers on governance questions surrounding the proposed

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:40.400
commercial arrangement. European football officials have described the plan as contrary to established

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:46.840
agreements. FIFA has not yet issued a detailed public response to the filing. The BBC reports

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:51.860
that UFFA views the attempted sale as a breach of governance rules the harmed European football

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:58.020
interests. FIFA maintains that its commercial decisions fall within its global mandate. European

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:04.120
football bodies argue that the move protects the sports traditional structures. FIFA frames its actions

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:10.080
as necessary modernization. The dispute highlights differing views on how revenue from major

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:16.040
tournaments should be allocated and controlled. UFFA's position rests on the claim that FIFA's commercial

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:21.460
plans violated agreements that protect the integrity and revenue flows of European leagues and clubs.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:27.140
FIFA's position rests on the claim that it holds sole authority over the World Cup's commercial

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:32.040
rights and that legal challenges from regional bodies undermine the sports global unity.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:38.460
Courts will determine whether the case proceeds to formal hearings. In South Africa, domestic sugar

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:44.300
producers are set to receive support through higher import duties. South Africa plans to raise the

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:49.700
benchmark price used to calculate duties on imported sugar. The adjustment would increase the

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:55.620
reference level against which import tariffs are assessed. Local growers, including units linked to

00:03:55.620 --> 00:04:01.260
associated British foods, have pressed for the change over several years. The move targets an

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:07.360
industry that has seen declining output amid cheaper overseas supplies. Bloomberg reports the change will

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:13.360
increase the cost of imported sugar and thereby support local growers. Industry groups argue the

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:18.980
adjustment restores fairness after years of cheap imports. Consumer advocates note that higher

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:24.960
duties could raise prices for households and food manufacturers. Domestic producers argue that the

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:30.160
benchmark adjustment counters below-cost imports that have reduced local output and employment.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:36.080
Importers and some retailers argue that the higher benchmark will raise input costs across the

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:42.620
food chain without addressing underlying productivity issues in local farming. The new benchmark level and

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:48.380
its effect on import volumes will be monitored by the industry. The Bank of Korea has raised interest

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:54.060
grades for the second consecutive meeting in response to rising inflation pressures. The decision follows

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:59.860
earlier tightening measures aimed at containing price growth. Central bank statements have emphasized the

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:05.860
need to safeguard household budgets from sustained cost increases. Commercial lenders are expected to pass

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:11.440
on the higher rates to borrowers. Nikkeh Asia notes that the central bank is acting to keep price

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:17.020
increases from eroding household purchasing power. Businesses have expressed concern that higher

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:22.840
borrowing costs could slow investment. The bank has stated that its decisions aim to maintain overall

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:28.960
economic stability. The sequence of hikes reflects ongoing monitoring of consumer price data and

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:34.820
external factors. Markets will watch the bank's next policy statement for signals on further moves.

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:40.340
Meanwhile, the Philippines has increased interest rates to address inflation linked to higher energy

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:46.480
costs, following developments involving Iran. The central bank cited imported price pressures from

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:52.040
fuel and related commodities as a primary driver. The rate increase is intended to limit the

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:57.960
pass-through of these costs into broader consumer prices. Officials have noted that the policy aims to

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:04.340
anchor longer-term inflation expectations. Nikkeh Asia reports the central bank's action as a direct

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:10.720
response to imported price pressures. Households face higher costs for fuel and electricity. The

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:16.140
bank has indicated that the move seeks to prevent broader price spirals. The decision aligns with

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:22.480
similar steps taken by other central banks facing external energy shocks. Officials will assess whether

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:29.080
additional adjustments are required. Now, some progress worth knowing about. Google, OpenAI,

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:35.120
Meta, and TikTok have agreed to work with Japanese authorities on measures to detect and block online

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:40.900
scams. The partnership includes commitments to share detection technologies and improve platform

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:46.900
safeguards. Japanese regulators have outlined plans to update relevant rules in parallel with the

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:52.700
technical cooperation. The initiative targets fraudulent schemes that have increased in volume across

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:58.920
digital channels. Nikkeh Asia reports the partnership as a concrete step to share detection tools and

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:04.260
limit the spread of fraudulent schemes. The companies will provide technical support while Japan

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:10.440
updates its regulatory framework. The collaboration addresses rising scam volumes that affect users

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:16.520
across age groups. Early discussions have focused on real-time identification of suspicious content

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:22.140
and accounts. The first joint detection systems are expected to be tested in the coming months.

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:28.220
To really understand the Hormuz fee proposal, most coverage assumes that major shipping lanes are

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:34.400
automatically free and open, yet coastal states have long-held limited rights to regulate or charge for

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:41.520
transit under specific conditions. The 1982 UN Convention on the Law of the Sea distinguishes between

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:47.060
transit passage through straits used for international navigation, and the broader rights a country may

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:53.700
exercise in its territorial waters. Turkey's long-standing boss Paris regime shows how a state can impose

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:59.360
safety, environmental, and tonnage-based fees without closing the route, provided the charges remain

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:05.400
reasonable and non-discriminatory. Iran's reference to this model suggests it is exploring a similar

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:11.160
bounded approach rather than a blanket toll. Knowing this distinction helps separate routine regulatory

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:23.680
updates from claims of sudden closure or blockade. When reports mention new fees, check whether the

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:29.960
opposite shore state has agreed to the framework. Historical examples from other straits demonstrate

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:36.100
that gradual implementation and bilateral consultation often determine whether such systems gain acceptance.

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:42.040
Observer's tracking the Hormuz proposal can compare the specific fee structure once published

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:47.600
against Turkey's published tariff schedules for comparable services. If you want to go deeper on

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:53.790
today's lead story, compare how Nikkeh Asia and Bloomberg frame economic policy changes in different

00:08:53.790 --> 00:09:00.510
regions. Tomorrow, watch for the first responses from Oman and major oil importers to Iran's proposed

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:06.930
fee system. That wraps up today's omnivue. Remember, the best informed people read more than one

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:12.930
perspective, and the calmest ones know that most days, somewhere, something is quietly getting better.

